'''Guled Badweyn''' (4 April 1483 – 8 October 1550) was a Burawi navigator, explorer, colonial administrator, and the first governor of [[Lacashe]]. He led the expedition that discovered the [[Badweyn Islands]] in 1512 and founded the first permanent Burawi settlement on the [[Abyala|Abyalan]] mainland in 1516. As governor, he directed the early expansion of [[Burawi Cusub]] into the Capuyaquiran region and organized the colonial campaigns that preceded the conquest of the [[Aruanãyára Empire]].

Guled was a member of the Burawi nobility and was related to the ruling dynasty through approximately five generations. He was an imperial expansionist who regarded the overseas extension of Burawi authority as a legitimate and beneficial mission. Although contemporary accounts described him as a fair and disciplined leader, he regarded non-Burawi peoples as politically subordinate to Burawa. He did not personally own slaves, but permitted slavery under colonial law during his administration of Lacashe.

In later Burawi political memory, Guled became a controversial figure. The socialist revolution and subsequent republican historiography labelled him an early symbol of Burawi imperialism and colonial violence. He is therefore remembered both as a major navigator and colonial founder and as one of the earliest architects of Burawi overseas rule.

== Early life ==

Guled was born on 4 April 1483 in [[Badalga]], a major Burawi naval and shipbuilding centre. His family belonged to the nobility and was related to the royal house, although not closely enough for Guled to be considered a claimant to the throne. His family connections gave him access to court, naval, and commercial institutions during a period of continued Burawi expansion.

He was educated in navigation, geography, military affairs, and colonial administration. Before receiving an independent command, he served in the Burawi maritime service and gained experience in open-ocean navigation. His political outlook was shaped by the expansionist ideology of the late imperial court. Guled believed that Burawa had the right and duty to establish overseas settlements, control strategic harbours, extend its trade routes, and bring foreign peoples under Burawi political and religious authority.

== Expeditions ==

=== Discovery of the Badweyn Islands ===

In 1512, at the age of 29, Guled commanded an expedition from the northern coast of the former [[Kingdom of Shedou|Shedou]] territory across approximately 3,300 kilometres of the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The expedition made landfall on an apparently uninhabited archipelago approximately 600 kilometres south of the Abyalan mainland.

Guled explored the islands for several months before returning to [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and reporting to King [[Dalgayan III]]. The archipelago was subsequently named the '''Badweyn Islands''' after him. The crown recognized the strategic importance of the discovery and granted Guled a royal charter to return with a larger expedition.

In 1515, his second expedition reached the islands and established a small Burawi outpost. The following year, the expedition continued northward to the eastern coast of Abyala.

=== Founding of Lacashe ===

In 1516, Guled's expedition made landfall on the eastern coast of Abyala near the southeastern coastal region later known as the Lacashe area. The settlement established there consisted of a fortified trading post, a harbour, a [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] mission, and supporting agricultural land. It became the first permanent Burawi settlement on the Abyalan mainland.

The settlement was later known as [[Guledga]], while the surrounding province became known as [[Lacashe]]. The colony occupied a strategically important position between the coastal communities, the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] in the interior lowlands, and the [[Aruanãyára Empire]] farther north in the highlands.

Guled's original settlement was intended to serve as a base for trade, exploration, and further expansion. During the following decades, Lacashe developed into the administrative centre of [[Burawi Cusub]], a major harbour, and the principal base for Burawi operations on the eastern coast of Abyala.

=== Later exploration ===

After establishing Lacashe, Guled returned to Burawa rather than remaining permanently in the colony. He continued to command exploratory voyages throughout the Burawi maritime sphere. These expeditions expanded Burawi knowledge of Abyalan coasts, the Capuyaquiran archipelagos, and the ocean routes connecting the colonies to Kaftia.

His experience in navigation, reconnaissance, military command, and overseas administration made him one of the crown's most trusted imperial officers. He later returned to Lacashe as its first civilian governor.

== First contact with Guajicutea ==

On 5 December 1520, Guled led a Burawi trade and reconnaissance expedition to [[Guajicutea]]. The expedition was travelling among the islands near Lacashe and entered the Capuyaquiran archipelago from the east. Guled's instructions were to record lands and armies and to assess possibilities for trade or expansion.

The expedition reached [[Yukubá]], Guajicutea's principal western port. Communication was attempted through a translator taken earlier from the Lacashe colony, who probably spoke an Aruanãyára or Lacashe dialect rather than Guaji. The translator could not bridge the linguistic distance between Burawi and Guaji, and the encounter remained limited.

Guled's troops left gold coins and maize as diplomatic gifts. The coins were unfamiliar to the Guaji, who used barter and weight-based exchange rather than minted currency. Maize, however, was already cultivated in Guajicutea after its introduction through Tapuyan trade during the early fifteenth century.

Guled described the coastal settlements as active and cosmopolitan ports with organized markets and connections to several island peoples. He considered them among the largest settlements he had yet encountered in the region. He also noted that Guaji vessels, although advanced within the Capuyaquiran region, were much smaller than Burawi ocean-going ships.

The expedition did not reach the inland capital of [[Tabakurí]] and did not meet the Cacique Má or the Warí. Guled therefore did not observe the full political or religious organization of Guajicutea. He departed the following day.

Guled's visit began the first direct contact between Burawa and Guajicutea. Burawi ships subsequently visited Guajicutea in a rotating trade pattern between 1523 and 1530. The trade brought paper to the island and contributed to the later transcription of the [[Kari Yabí]] chronicle.

== Governor of Lacashe ==

=== Civilian reorganization ===

In 1535, during the reign of [[Faarax III]], Lacashe was reorganized from a naval-administered outpost into a civilian colonial government. Guled became its first governor. Before the reorganization, the settlement had been managed directly by the Burawi navy as a fortified resupply station and base for exploration.

As governor, Guled received broad authority from Burawa to conduct colonial expansion. His responsibilities included civil administration, military coordination, relations with indigenous polities, trade regulation, settlement, Church affairs, and intelligence gathering. He remained the senior Burawi official in the region until his death.

Guled's administration was centralized and authoritarian. He maintained strict discipline among Burawi officials and soldiers, but contemporary reports described him as fair in the distribution of supplies, rewards, and punishments. His fairness did not imply political equality. Guled believed that non-Burawi peoples were subordinate to Burawi authority and that resistance to colonial rule could legitimately be suppressed.

=== The Great Pestilence ===

Guled governed during the Great Pestilence, a regional smallpox pandemic that affected indigenous populations from approximately 1525 to 1548. The disease spread through the maritime trade and military networks linking the colonies and island communities. Guajicutea suffered particularly severe losses, with approximately thirty percent of its population dying by 1538.

The epidemic weakened indigenous political systems, disrupted agriculture and trade, and reduced resistance to colonial expansion. Guled's administration did not necessarily initiate the spread of the disease, but Lacashe's ships, trade routes, military movements, and mission network formed part of the system through which it travelled. Later historians have treated this period as one in which Burawi expansion benefited from an epidemic catastrophe among the indigenous population.

=== First Capuyaquiran War ===

Guled was the senior Burawi official responsible for the First Capuyaquiran War of 1540–1543. The conflict began when former Guaji tributaries, including the Murani, Dukuri, and Barani islands, resisted Guajicutean authority during the crisis caused by the Great Pestilence.

Guled delegated the field command to a subordinate Burawi military officer, but he authorized the intervention, directed its political objectives, coordinated with Guajicutea, and controlled the colonial resources committed to the campaign. The war was presented as a joint effort to restore order and return the islands to Guajicutean obedience.

Following the defeat of the island resistance, the conquered territories were not returned to Guajicutea. They were reorganized under Burawi authority as the [[Badweyn Islands]]. The settlement transformed a regional tributary revolt into a permanent colonial acquisition and attached Guled's name to the territory created by the campaign.

=== Second Capuyaquiran War ===

Guled's governorship also coincided with the [[Second Capuyaquiran War]] of 1538–1548. During this conflict, Volisania expanded into western Capuyaquiran while Burawa consolidated its own position in the east. The war involved colonial garrisons, indigenous allies, competing trade networks, and disputes left unresolved by the [[Abyala Charter]] of 1537.

The charter divided much of mainland Abyala between Burawa and Volisania but left several island and southern coastal territories insufficiently regulated. Guled used this uncertainty to justify Burawi expansion around Lacashe and the Capuyaquiran Sea. His administration treated control of the islands as necessary for the security of Burawi shipping and the future expansion of the colony.

=== Prelude to the Aruanãyára conquest ===

The conquest of the [[Aruanãyára Empire]] began in 1547, during Guled's governorship. Although he did not command the campaign in the field and died before its completion, he helped establish the system from which the conquest was organized.

Under Guled, Lacashe developed into a military and administrative base with fortified ports, supply depots, colonial roads, intelligence networks, Church missions, and alliances with local groups. These institutions allowed Burawi forces to move from coastal settlement toward inland conquest.

Guled authorized the initial reconnaissance and logistical preparations for the campaign and coordinated the use of colonial ships, soldiers, supplies, and indigenous auxiliaries. After his death, his successors and their military commanders continued the conquest. The Aruanãyára Empire was ultimately defeated and annexed by approximately 1575.

Guled was therefore not the sole conqueror of Aruanãyára, but he was one of the principal architects of the system that made the conquest possible.

== Policies and character ==

Guled was an imperial expansionist. He believed that Burawa was entitled to establish political authority over overseas peoples and that colonial rule would bring order, trade, Christianity, and prosperity. He preferred controlled expansion through treaties, alliances, missions, and regulated trade when those methods produced submission, but he supported military conquest when they failed.

He did not personally own slaves and never married. Nevertheless, he allowed slavery under colonial law during his administration of Lacashe. His personal refusal to own slaves did not lead him to oppose the colonial institution. He accepted the subordination of non-Burawi populations and enforced the laws of the colonial state.

Guled's contemporaries therefore remembered him as a disciplined and fair administrator, while later indigenous and socialist historians emphasized that his administrative order existed to serve conquest. His government opposed unauthorized violence when it threatened colonial stability, but it did not recognize the political equality or sovereignty of the peoples subjected to Burawi rule.

== Death and legacy ==

Guled died in Lacashe on 8 October 1550, at the age of 67, after contracting malaria. He died in office, three years after the beginning of the Aruanãyára conquest and during the period in which Burawi expansion was rapidly extending across Abyala and the Capuyaquiran region.

He left no wife or known children. His political legacy passed through the colonial institutions and officials who succeeded him rather than through a hereditary family administration.

In imperial Burawi histories, Guled was long celebrated as the discoverer of the Badweyn Islands, founder of Lacashe, and pioneer of Burawi expansion into Abyala. The socialist revolution in Burawa rejected this interpretation and labelled him an early symbol of imperial evil. Socialist and post-revolutionary histories associate him with the displacement of indigenous peoples, the spread of colonial rule, the expansion of slavery, and the epidemic conditions that accompanied overseas conquest.

Modern assessments generally treat Guled as both a major navigator and a principal architect of Burawi colonialism. He did not personally complete the conquest of the Aruanãyára Empire, but the colonial state, military infrastructure, and expansionist policy established during his governorship enabled his successors to complete it.

